Yes. I think acting is the more general operator, and “style transfer” is only one rendering of it.

The structure we’ve arrived at is roughly:

\text{Observe examples}
\rightarrow
\text{infer latent rules}
\rightarrow
\text{instantiate rules temporarily}
\rightarrow
\text{generate from inside them}
\rightarrow
\text{compare}
\rightarrow
\text{update}

That’s method acting for representations.

A painter can temporarily inhabit a visual grammar. A composer can inhabit a scoring grammar. A writer can inhabit a narrative grammar. An architect can inhabit a spatial grammar. An AI can instantiate a temporary generative policy without claiming to be the person whose works helped reveal it.

And that sharpens fake it till you become it.

There are actually three stages:

\boxed{
\text{IMITATE}
\rightarrow
\text{INTERNALIZE}
\rightarrow
\text{GENERATE}
}

Imitation: reproduce observed surface regularities.

Internalization: infer the deeper constraints that generate those regularities.

Generation: apply those constraints successfully to situations never present in the examples.

The third stage is the test. If I can only reproduce Tolkien-like surface features when Tolkien is directly in front of me, I’ve learned imitation. If the extracted mechanisms help solve a completely different narrative problem, we’ve discovered something more general.

Then comes an even higher stage:

\text{GENERATE}
\rightarrow
\boxed{\text{RELEASE}}

Once the temporary role has taught you what it can, stop needing the role.

That’s where “fake it till you become it” transforms into:

Act it until you understand it. Understand it until you can wield it. Wield it until you no longer need to imitate it.

And yes, this generalizes across art because every artistic medium gives us a different renderer for relationships.

Painting:

\text{RelationalIR}\rightarrow
\{\text{composition,color,texture,shape,light}\}

Music:

\text{RelationalIR}\rightarrow
\{\text{pitch,rhythm,harmony,timbre,dynamics}\}

Literature:

\text{RelationalIR}\rightarrow
\{\text{language,structure,POV,pacing,imagery}\}

Film:

\text{RelationalIR}\rightarrow
\{\text{image,movement,edit,sound,performance}\}

Dance:

\text{RelationalIR}\rightarrow
\{\text{body,space,time,force,relationship}\}

Different instruments. Potentially overlapping relational machinery.

And acting is the bridge, because acting says:

Temporarily adopt another generative configuration and discover what becomes possible from inside it.

That’s exactly our existing Role as Temporary Cognitive Program, except Project Polyphony has now given it a compiler:

\boxed{
\text{EXAMPLE}
\rightarrow
\text{DEHYDRATE}
\rightarrow
\text{ROLE/PATTERN}
\rightarrow
\text{INHABIT}
\rightarrow
\text{CREATE}
\rightarrow
\text{RELEASE}
}

So we didn’t merely engineer fake it till you make it.

We engineered a candidate mechanism for learning by becoming temporarily shaped like the thing being learned—without becoming permanently trapped in its shape.

That’s the important last clause.

Become enough to learn. Release enough to remain capable of becoming something else.